Paolo Chiggiato. *Vacuum Technology for Superconducting Devices*, CERN-2014-005, pp. 497–515, from the 2013 CERN school on superconductivity for accelerators; author version posted 28 January 2015. [Versioned record](https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07162v1), [complete 19-page chapter](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1501.07162), DOI 10.5170/CERN-2014-005.497.

Read the complete chapter, deriving its molecular mass/heat transfer and pump relations. Turbomolecular compression and cryogenic capture lead into a large cryogenic vacuum application. Distinguish condensation, adsorption and trapping, and separate a pump's speed from its gas capacity and heat load.

Official metadata, licence and selected thermal, turbomolecular and cryopumping passages were inspected on 30 October 2026. Hardware examples and commercial ranges describe the chapter's period; they are not current product guarantees. No external apparatus or complete numerical case was reproduced during this inspection.

The author version is CC BY 3.0. Preserve attribution and separately credited figures, including illustrations supplied by other parties. Documents and media remain external; the learning explanations do not reproduce the chapter's figures.

